<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mogilev</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mahilyow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mogilev, or Mahilyow, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, it grew into an important trading town and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights in 1577. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772, and in 1915-1917 it was the headquarters of the Russian army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mogilev.jpg" alt="Mogilev" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liashko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahilio%C5%AD,_ratu%C5%A1a._%D0%9C%D0%B0%D0%B3%D1%96%D0%BB%D1%91%D1%9E,_%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B0_(2020).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The city stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, about 76 kilometres from the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Smolensk Oblast, and was first mentioned in historical records in 1267. Its name may derive from the Russian mogila, grave, and lev, lion. According to folk legend it was named after the grave of a young peasant, known as the Tomb of the Lion, around which a fortress was built. Its founding has also been linked to the Galician prince Leo I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 14th century Mogilev belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> of 1569 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>, where it was known as Mohylew. In the 16th and 17th centuries it was one of the main nodes of the east–west and north–south trade routes.</p><p>In 1654, during the Russo-Polish War, the townsmen agreed to surrender to the Russians if the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were expelled and their property divided. Tsar Aleksei Mikhailovitch accepted, but Russian troops led the Jews to the outskirts of the town and massacred them. Lithuanian forces besieged the city in 1655 and 1660. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s forces set it afire in 1708, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>.</p><p>In 1897 the Russian census counted 21,500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> among 41,100 inhabitants, over half the total. In 1919 Soviet Russia incorporated the city into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a>. In 1938 the Belarusian leadership decided to move the capital here from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a>, which lay too close to the Polish border, and a government building was raised in 1938–1940. German forces took the city on 26 July 1941 and held it until 28 June 1944. The Jews were confined in a ghetto and systematically murdered by police and SS personnel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a> witnessed the shooting of 279 Jews on 23 October 1941. A plan for a death camp was dropped in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maly-trostenets">Maly Trostenets</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mogilev is the administrative centre of Mogilev Region and the third-largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it became a large metallurgical centre with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> mills and factories making cranes, cars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractors</a>, and it has an inland port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. Its convent of St. Nicholas has a cathedral built in 1668, with the original iconostasis, bell tower, walls and gates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mogilev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
